package mop;

import java.security.Key;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * Key
 *
 * A JavaMOP specification of the correct usage of the interface java.security.Key.
 * The file is named for the specification and not for the rule's own word: read alone,
 * `KeySpec` suggests java.security.spec.KeySpec, which is a different type entirely. The
 * name is kept because the ledger pairs a rule to `<Rule>Spec.mop` by convention
 * (`gh105_expert_ledger.py:438-448`), and an exception in the pairing code would be worse
 * than a name that needs one sentence.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/Key.crysl
 */
KeySpec(Key key) {

   // The bytes `getEncoded()` returned, held from the event that observed them to the
   // handler that may write about them. A handler sees no event parameter and the clause
   // says where the predicate is established, so the two have to be joined by a field --
   // the same shape `SecretKeySpec.mop:27` uses for the same clause.
   byte[] stagedKeyMaterial;

   // The whole of this specification: reading a key's encoding. The rule ENSURES
   // `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial] after GetEnc` (Key.crysl:14) and states no
   // CONSTRAINTS and no REQUIRES, so nothing here translates a constraint and nothing here
   // accuses -- this event has no code in `codes.csv` and gains none, exactly as its
   // `SecretKey` twin has none.
   //
   // What it does is bridge a copy. `getEncoded()` returns a fresh clone on every call and
   // the store is keyed on object identity, so without this event no reader of the returned
   // bytes could ever see what was established about the key they came out of.
   //
   // THE SECOND INTERFACE-OWNED POINTCUT OF THE SET, AND THE ONE THAT OVERLAPS ANOTHER.
   // `Key` is the supertype of `SecretKey`, `PublicKey` and `PrivateKey` (measured on the
   // api30 archive: `SecretKeySpec implements KeySpec, SecretKey` and
   // `SecretKey extends Key`), and the owner matcher keeps the trailing `+` and walks
   // interfaces (`PointcutMatcher:322-345` through `AndroidClassIndex.walkAncestors`, the
   // R5 measurement). So `Key+.getEncoded()` matches every receiver
   // `SecretKeySpec.mop`'s `SecretKey+.getEncoded()` matches, and a `SecretKey`'s encoding
   // is observed twice, once by each specification. That double monitoring is deliberate
   // and costs nothing at the store: `ensure` is idempotent per (property, identity,
   // values), so the second write of the same tuple adds nothing.
   //
   // What it would have cost is the reason this body carries a guard the rule does not
   // state. `SecretKeySpec.mop` stages only when the key's own origin was observed, and
   // that guard is not decoration: it is what refuses to launder key material through a
   // copy -- hard-coded bytes, accused at their own `SecretKeySpec` construction, handing
   // their encoding on as prepared material for a second `SecretKeySpec` that would then be
   // silent (researcher decision, 2026-08-22, measured by
   // `SecretKeySpec-laundered-material.txt`). Written to the letter of `Key.crysl`, which
   // states no REQUIRES, this file would prepare that material unconditionally through the
   // Key monitor and cancel the refusal without a line of either specification changing.
   //
   // So the guard is mirrored here rather than dropped, and generalised to the shape `Key`
   // has: a key's origin is observed when the store carries `generatedKey` for it with its
   // own algorithm (the `SecretKey` route, `SecretKeySpec.crysl:27` and
   // `KeyGenerator.crysl:32`), or `generatedPubkey`, or `generatedPrivkey`
   // (`KeyPair.crysl:24-28`, `KeyStore.crysl:62`). Any one of the three answering SATISFIED
   // is an origin. This is a MOP-MAIS-RESTRITIVO divergence against the rule, recorded as
   // such, and the twin of the one `SecretKeySpec.mop` already carries against
   // `SecretKey.crysl` -- which is the point: two specifications watching one call must
   // agree about what it means, or the looser of them decides.
   //
   // The verdict is three-valued and here the three collapse into two, for the reason the
   // twin states: the read governs a write instead of a report, so NOT_OBSERVED and
   // VIOLATED both stage nothing and the difference between them has no reader to tell it
   // to.
   event ge1 after(Key k) returning(byte[] material):
      call(public byte[] Key+.getEncoded()) &&
      target(k) {
        boolean originObserved =
            PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_KEY, k, k.getAlgorithm()) == PredicateVerdict.SATISFIED
            || PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_PUBLIC_KEY, k) == PredicateVerdict.SATISFIED
            || PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_PRIVATE_KEY, k) == PredicateVerdict.SATISFIED;
        if (originObserved) {
            stagedKeyMaterial = material;
        }
   }

   // `GetEnc*` is the whole of the rule's ORDER (:11), so every sequence of `getEncoded()`
   // calls is accepted and no `@fail` handler is written: there is no sequence to refuse,
   // which is why this file, like `SecretKeySpec.mop`, appears in `codes.csv` with no rows
   // at all.
   //
   // The clause carries `after GetEnc`, so it is established at the states `ge` leads to;
   // the `ere` is `ge1*`, whose only alias is `match` over its accepting states. Read from
   // the generated monitor the transition row for `ge1` is {0, 1} and the match category is
   // `nextstate == 0`, so the states after `ge` and the accepting states are the same
   // single state and the handler below is both routes at once (INV-INS-134).
   ere : ge1*

   // Runs after every `ge1`, which is what the clause needs: each call returns its own copy
   // and each copy has to be marked separately. The staged array is consumed here or
   // discarded, so a call whose key origin was not observed never leaves a marking behind
   // for the next one.
   @match {
     if (stagedKeyMaterial != null) {
         PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.PREPARED_KEY_MATERIAL, stagedKeyMaterial);
         stagedKeyMaterial = null;
     }
   }

}
